course: dictionary
lesson: 4165
title: Moving the goalposts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A goalpost is one of the two upright posts at each end of a goal in football, rugby and hockey. The ball must go between them to score.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. «Move the goalposts» means changing the rules or the target unfairly once something has already started. The goal is moved so that it is harder to win.
explain: To spot it, ask: did the rules change after the game began? «Changing the target each time someone meets it is moving the goalposts.» Fair rules are clear before the race starts.
fact: A goalpost is one of two upright posts that mark the sides of a goal.
fact: In football, rugby and hockey, the ball must go between the goalposts to score.
fact: In rugby and Australian football, the ball can go between or over tall posts.
fact: «Move the goalposts» means changing the rules or the target unfairly once something has started.
fact: Goalposts must be fixed firmly to the ground so they cannot fall over and hurt someone.
exercise: What is a goalpost? | *One of the two upright posts that mark the sides of a goal | A net that catches the ball | A painted line at the edge of the pitch | A player who stands in goal
exercise: «Changing the target each time someone meets it is moving the goalposts.» What does moving the goalposts mean here? | Scoring a goal from far away | *Changing the rules or target unfairly after it has started | Putting the ball in the net | Setting up new posts before a fair game begins
exercise: Tom says, «Only goals from the left side count» after Mia has already scored from the right. What is Tom doing? | *Moving the goalposts | Scoring a goal | Keeping goal | Passing the ball to a friend
test: Where must the ball go to score a goal in football? | Out of play behind the line | Into the crowd | Over the fence at the side of the pitch | *Between the goalposts
test: Ella's class agrees three rules before the game. Halfway through, Ella adds a fourth rule that only she has to follow. Is she moving the goalposts? | No, adding rules always makes a game better | No, goalposts cannot be moved at all | *Yes, she is changing the rules unfairly after the game has started | Yes, but only in rugby
exam: Which best explains «move the goalposts»? | *Change the rules unfairly after something has started | Stand still and wait for the whistle | Kick the ball hard into the net | Paint new lines on the pitch
